    not_duplicate_of: []  # 53b1
    pr_ref:  # 53b1
    description: "Filed 2026-04-22 after WI-lokuv auto-import landed.\n\nThe auto-import design shipped in WI-lokuv (commit f46c0610d) assumes user-supplied project-local taint catalogs override auto-derived defaults by (module, name, kind). The merge logic (_merge_with_user_override) exists and works. But the CLI has no way to POINT at a user-supplied YAML — load_builtin_taint_catalog() only scans the directory inside the installed package (Path(__file__).parent / 'taint_sinks' etc.), which on a PyPI install is inside site-packages and is not a reasonable place for a user to drop a repo-local file.\n\nConsequence: as of today, an end-user running 'verify-claims' on their own repo gets paranoid defaults (every fs_write / net_send / subprocess / env_write / ipc_send / browser_storage_write primitive is a sink at trust_level=untrusted) with NO way to:\n\n- add a new trust zone for a project-specific sink (PlazaFlow-style CRDT relays, VPN TUN interfaces, etc.)\n- declare a sanitizer so tainted data can legitimately reach a sink (e.g. encryption before fs_write)\n- lower trust_level for a sink that's actually safe in context\n- add a domain-specific taint source label\n\nADR-0017 documented the project-local extension story as core to the design, but that story is currently unreachable through the CLI.\n\nCode today has a placeholder comment acknowledging the gap (cli.py:3503-3504):\n\n    # Also load project-local taint catalogs if specified in claims file\n    # (future: --taint-sources, --taint-sinks, --taint-sanitizers args)\n\nProposed fix:\n\n1. Add --taint-sources PATH, --taint-sinks PATH, --taint-sanitizers PATH flags to the verify-claims argparse, repeatable (action='append') so users can supply multiple.\n\n2. Resolve each PATH as either a single YAML file or a directory of YAMLs. If directory, glob('*.yaml') like the built-in loader.\n\n3. Also accept these under a new top-level key in the claims YAML (something like 'extra_catalogs: {sources: [...], sinks: [...], sanitizers: [...]}'), since many users will already be pointing at a claims file and a single location for configuration is cleaner than repeated CLI flags.\n\n4. Build a public helper that combines built-in auto-import + user YAMLs. load_builtin_taint_catalog() currently returns the merged catalog; extend it (or add a sibling load_full_taint_catalog(extra_source_paths, extra_sink_paths, extra_sanitizer_paths)) that applies user overrides on top of the auto-derived defaults. Override semantics stay the same: user entry wins on (module, name, kind) match.\n\n5. When the override kicks in to change trust_level or zone, verify-claims output should make it visible somehow ('source: project-local override' in the finding) so users know their override took effect.\n\n6. Documentation: once this ships, update docs/hypergumbo-spec.md §19 to describe the CLI flags and the claims-file integration. The stub doc pass landing in the WI-lokuv-followup doc PR describes only the current (built-in-only) state.\n\nTests:\n\n- unit: verify-claims CLI with explicit --taint-sources PATH loads and merges user sources\n- unit: entry from user YAML matching a built-in auto-import's (module, name, kind) overrides trust_level\n- integration: on a fixture repo, a user sink in a new 'relay' zone is respected by verify-claims even though 'relay' isn't in AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP\n- ensure per-package coverage stays 100%\n\nNo schema changes to the taint_sinks/taint_sources/taint_sanitizers YAML format — this is purely a CLI plumbing task on top of existing parse functions.\n\nBlocks: any real end-user adoption of verify-claims on a repo other than hypergumbo's own. Until this ships, the feature is effectively 'paranoid default with no escape hatch.'"  # 53b1
    fields: {}  # 53b1
